+     * because it really is part of the node's source text; an error range is a pointer into the
+     * source for a human to read, so it prefers content bytes. Lexer applies the same trimming to
+     * the unterminated-comment error.
+     *
+     * Which of two things that means depends on what the range holds:
+     *  * With at least one content byte in it, the range shrinks onto content: the start moves
+     *    forward past leading terminators, the end moves back past trailing ones, and startLine
+     *    and endLine are corrected by the line breaks the range gave up.
